If you know what ports are open and what ports are not,
you can run ssh on a non-standard port or just use IP
Tables to redirect some other port to 22.  This assumes
that there's at least one port forwarded (but not proxied)
that you don't need for anything else.  Ports that are
frequently unblocked even when almost everything
else is blocked include 53 and 443.

You still need an ssh client on every system, but
that's significantly less of a hassle than trying to
install a newsreader everywhere.  Unix systems
of course all come with one built in, and even for
Windows systems Putty is much less annoying
to set up than e.g. Thunderbird.

This is what I would do if I could find an nntp
server that consistently works, but my ISP does
not provide that and the open public ones I've
